statements.proof_tex:
Put \(T_n=N_n+G_n\) and \(X_n=\widehat\tau_n-\tau_n\).  By Definition~\ref{def:reveal-martingale}, \(\mathcal F_{n,0}\) is trivial, whereas \(\mathcal F_{n,T_n}\) reveals the first \(N_n\) entries of \(\mathbf U_n^{\mathrm{perm}}\), which determine every realized group, and all \(G_n\) entries of \(\mathbf L_n^{\mathrm{perm}}\), which determine every treatment label.  Thus \(X_n\) is \(\mathcal F_{n,T_n}\)-measurable.  Theorem~\ref{thm:exact-pame-variance} gives \(\mathbb E X_n=0\), where this and all subsequent expectations are under the two-stage randomization law.  The conditional-expectation differences in Definition~\ref{def:reveal-martingale} therefore telescope exactly:
\[
 \sum_{r=1}^{T_n}D_{n,r}
 =\mathbb E[X_n\mid\mathcal F_{n,T_n}]
  -\mathbb E[X_n\mid\mathcal F_{n,0}]
 =X_n.
\tag{1}
\]

Membership in \(\mathcal C_{\mathrm{dense}}\) supplies scaled-variance nondegeneracy.  Consequently, for all sufficiently large \(n\),
\[
 \sigma_n^2\ge \frac{c_\sigma}{2G_n}>0.
\tag{2}
\]
Lemma~\ref{lem:reveal-increment-bound} gives a deterministic almost-sure bound on every \(D_{n,r}\), so the martingale differences are square integrable.  The same lemma derives from group-count growth, stable allocation, bounded potential outcomes, and (2) that, for every \(\varepsilon>0\),
\[
 \frac{1}{\sigma_n^2}\sum_{r=1}^{T_n}
 \mathbb E\!\left[
 D_{n,r}^2\mathbf 1\{|D_{n,r}|>\varepsilon\sigma_n\}
 \mathrel{\big|}\mathcal F_{n,r-1}
 \right]\xrightarrow{p}0.
\tag{3}
\]
The theorem's additional antecedent, which is not a membership condition for \(\mathcal C_{\mathrm{dense}}\), is
\[
 \frac{Q_n}{\sigma_n^2}
 =\frac{1}{\sigma_n^2}\sum_{r=1}^{T_n}
 \mathbb E[D_{n,r}^2\mid\mathcal F_{n,r-1}]
 \xrightarrow{p}1.
\tag{4}
\]
Equations (3)--(4) are respectively the conditional Lindeberg and predictable-quadratic-variation hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:martingale-array-clt}, applied to the array \(D_{n,r}/\sigma_n\).  That lemma and (1) yield
\[
 \frac{\widehat\tau_n-\tau_n}{\sigma_n}
 =\frac{1}{\sigma_n}\sum_{r=1}^{T_n}D_{n,r}
 \rightsquigarrow\mathcal N(0,1).
\]
All probabilities and conditional expectations in this argument refer only to \((\mathbf A_n,\mathbf Z_n)\).  The complete schedule \(Y_{i,n}(z,A)\) is fixed, so the convergence is pointwise and design-based, not a superpopulation limit.

